\section{Choosing an objective}{

A candidate node set \eqn{S} of \code{n} nodes carrying total suprathreshold edge
weight \code{w} is scored by

\describe{
\item{\code{"generalized"}}{\eqn{w / n^{2\lambda}}, the default. This is the
adaptive density function \eqn{f(S; \lambda_W) = |W(S)| / |S|^{\lambda_W}}
of Wu et al. (2022), and equivalently the \eqn{\ell_0} graph norm
shrinkage criterion \eqn{\log\|U\|_1 - \lambda_0 \log\|U\|_0} of Chen et
al. (2023), which rewards edge weight within a subnetwork while
penalizing its size.}
\item{\code{"density"}}{\eqn{w / \binom{n}{2}}, pure edge density, ignoring
\code{lambda}. With no size floor this degenerates to the single densest pair
of nodes, so use it only with a substantial \code{min_size}.}
}
}

\section{Parameterization of lambda}{

The \code{lambda} used here equals \eqn{\lambda_0} of Chen et al. (2023) and half
of \eqn{\lambda_W} of Wu et al. (2022), whose exponent runs over [1, 2].
Concretely, \code{lambda = 0.5} (\eqn{\lambda_W = 1}) gives the degree density
\eqn{f_1}, the objective of Charikar (2000), and \code{lambda = 1}
(\eqn{\lambda_W = 2}) gives the area density \eqn{f_2}. Larger values favour
smaller and denser subnetworks; \code{lambda = 0} places every node in one
subnetwork. Values between 0.5 and 0.7 are the usual working range, and
\code{\link[=tune_subnet]{tune_subnet()}} selects a value from the data when none is supplied.
}

